[THOUGHT] Repeated “Not specified” responses for semen analysis and HSG suggest these tests are either not yet performed *or* not available in this clinical environment. Since the Analyst’s prior replies included “Not available in this clinical environment” for some tests (though not explicitly stated here), and given the turn limit, I must pivot to the next most informative *available* test — one that is both feasible and highly discriminating. With ovulation confirmed (progesterone normal) and no red flags for endocrine disease, the remaining key domains are:  
- **Ovarian reserve**, which helps distinguish age-related or early diminished reserve from unexplained infertility;  
- **Thyroid function**, as subclinical hypothyroidism can impair fertility without overt symptoms;  
- **Prolactin**, since hyperprolactinemia can suppress GnRH pulsatility and cause subtle luteal phase defects despite normal mid-luteal progesterone.  

Among these, **TSH** is the most universally available, inexpensive, and high-yield screening test — it’s recommended in all infertility evaluations (ASRM/ESHRE) and abnormal results would directly redirect management. It also has no contraindications and requires no timing coordination.

[TEST] Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) level

